{"id":"why-we-coach","title":"Why we coach","minutes":6,"tracks":["coach","volunteer"],"summary":"Start with the individual child and the environment they experience.","sections":[{"heading":"The individual child comes first","html":"<p>The individual child is at the centre of everything we do. Our purpose is to create an environment where each child enjoys playing, feels involved, and develops an understanding of the game. Every coaching decision starts with the child in front of us: their needs, their experience, and their development.</p><p>The session, the activity, and the team are there to support each child's learning. We aim to cultivate a love for the game and develop an understanding of it in every child.</p>"},{"heading":"Start with the environment","html":"<p>Your coaches guide describes a child-centred, engaging and enjoyable environment. For a coach, that purpose becomes real through everyday choices: how we welcome children, how much opportunity they have to play, and how we respond when they try something difficult.</p><p>A session can look organised from the sideline while a child spends most of it waiting, listening, or worrying about mistakes. Notice the experience from the child's point of view as well as your own. A session working well for most of the group is a reason to check who still needs something different.</p>"},{"heading":"Intent over outcome is our value","html":"<p>A child can make a thoughtful decision and still lose the ball. Another can score without understanding why the opportunity appeared. The result gives us information, but it does not tell us everything about the child's thinking.</p><p>Intent over outcome is a coaching value: recognise the decision, courage and purpose behind an attempt. It also gives parents a useful way to look beyond the score. For example: ‘You spotted that space and tried to use it. What could you try next time?’</p>"},{"heading":"Take it into a game","html":"<p>Watch one child choosing to dribble, protect, share, steal or score. Notice whether they have room to make a decision. Recognise one purposeful attempt, then let them try again. Ask what they enjoyed and use that answer to shape your next response.</p>"}],"mediaIds":["environment"],"quiz":[{"id":"why-we-coach-1","prompt":"A child spots space, attempts to dribble into it and loses the ball. What would you say?","options":["You spotted a space. What could you try next time?","Only try that if you know it will work.","We only celebrate successful moves."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Recognising the intention connects feedback to the child’s thinking, even when the outcome disappoints."},{"id":"why-we-coach-2","prompt":"Most players enjoy a game, but one child rarely gets involved. What next?","options":["Continue because the majority are happy.","Notice that child’s experience and ask what would help.","Label the child unmotivated."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Group success does not tell us what each child needs."},{"id":"why-we-coach-3","prompt":"A parent asks why you encouraged an unsuccessful attempt. What best explains the value?","options":["Results never matter.","Research guarantees the next attempt will succeed.","We recognise purposeful decisions and courage while helping children learn from what happened."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Intent over outcome counters outcome bias. It is a value for interpreting an attempt, not a statistical claim or guarantee."}],"takeaway":"Choose one child to understand better and recognise one decision worth encouraging.","sourceRefs":[{"sourceId":"guide-2","page":2},{"sourceId":"guide-2","page":22},{"sourceId":"guide-2","page":23},{"sourceId":"guide-2","page":24},{"sourceId":"guide-2","page":39}]}
